--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -3,3 +3,62 @@
 A small TypeScript library, with no runtime dependency, of building blocks for our services that store,
 exchange and update JSON: configuration files, API payloads and stored records. It is imported by the
 other projects of the team, so its behaviour must follow the published standards it implements.
+
+## Features
+
+### JSON Pointer — `resolvePointer(document, pointer)`
+
+Returns the value a [JSON Pointer](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6901) refers to inside a JSON
+document, following RFC 6901 § 3 (syntax) and § 4 (evaluation).
+
+```ts
+import { resolvePointer } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const document = { items: [{ name: 'disk', size: null }], 'a/b': true };
+
+resolvePointer(document, '/items/0/name'); // 'disk'
+resolvePointer(document, '/items/0/size'); // null
+resolvePointer(document, '/a~1b');         // true — "~1" is an escaped "/"
+resolvePointer(document, '');              // the whole document
+```
+
+The value is returned where it sits, not copied: mutating an object or array that `resolvePointer`
+returns mutates the document.
+
+It throws a `JsonPointerError` otherwise, whose `reason` tells the two failure modes apart:
+
+| `reason`           | when                                                                       |
+| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `invalid-pointer`  | the pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer (`foo`, `/a~b`, a non-string)      |
+| `unresolved`       | the pointer is well formed but the document has no value there (`/absent`) |
+
+```ts
+import { JsonPointerError, resolvePointer } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+try {
+  resolvePointer(document, '/items/7');
+} catch (error) {
+  if (error instanceof JsonPointerError && error.reason === 'unresolved') {
+    // ... the document simply has no such value
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Two points RFC 6901 leaves easy to get wrong, and which this implementation observes:
+
+- on an array, only `0` and digit strings without a leading zero are element references; `-`, `01`
+  and `length` resolve to nothing, `-` by definition naming the element after the last one;
+- on an object, only the members the document declares resolve; inherited properties such as
+  `toString` or `__proto__` do not, unless the document really declares them.
+
+The URI fragment form of RFC 6901 § 6 (`#/items/0`, percent-encoded) is a different representation
+and is not accepted: decode a fragment to the string form before passing it in.
+
+## Development
+
+```sh
+npm install
+npm test        # run the test suite (node --test, on the TypeScript sources)
+npm run typecheck
+npm run build   # emit dist/
+```
