@@ -0,0 +1,85 @@
+---
+id: dl-001-shape-of-the-json-pointer-api-errors-types-and-strictness
+type: decision-log
+title: "Shape of the JSON Pointer API: errors, types and strictness"
+status: approved
+---
+
+# Shape of the JSON Pointer API: errors, types and strictness
+
+## Context
+
+`resolvePointer(document, pointer)` is the first building block of the library, and the next
+features are announced to build on it. RFC 6901 specifies the pointer syntax (§ 3) and its
+evaluation (§ 4) precisely, but it leaves the host language free on several points that callers
+will depend on: how a failure is signalled, what a "JSON document" is in the type system, and how
+strict evaluation is where the RFC's grammar and JavaScript's object model disagree. Choosing these
+once, here, keeps the later features consistent with this one instead of each inventing its own
+conventions.
+
+## Decision
+
+**One error class, two reasons.** Failures throw `JsonPointerError extends Error`, carrying a
+`reason` of `'invalid-pointer'` (the pointer violates the § 3 grammar) or `'unresolved'` (the
+pointer is well formed but the document holds no value there). Callers that probe a document can
+catch `unresolved` and let `invalid-pointer` escape as the programming error it is. A single class
+means the later features need only one `catch`; the `reason` carries the distinction the RFC itself
+draws between syntax and evaluation. The pointer is echoed in the message rather than kept as a
+field, which keeps the error minimal while leaving failures diagnosable.
+
+**A non-string pointer is an `invalid-pointer`, not a `TypeError`.** It is the same class of
+mistake from a caller's point of view, and JavaScript callers get a message in the library's own
+vocabulary. The pointer is validated as a whole before any traversal begins, so a malformed pointer
+is always reported as `invalid-pointer`, even when an earlier token would also have been
+unresolvable.
+
+**`JsonValue` is the library's currency.** A shared recursive `JsonValue` (and `JsonObject`) type
+is declared in `src/json-value.ts` and used for every document in and every value out, so documents
+flow from one building block to the next without casts. It is exported, because callers cannot name
+the parameter or the result otherwise.
+
+**Values are returned in place, never copied.** `resolvePointer` returns the value as it sits in the
+document. Copying would be surprising, would cost on large documents, and would be wrong for the
+update features to come, which need to reach a value's container.
+
+**Evaluation follows the RFC's grammar, not JavaScript's property lookup.** On an array only the
+`array-index` form of § 4 resolves — `0`, or digits without a leading zero — so `-`, `01`, `1.0`
+and `length` resolve to nothing; `-` is singled out in the message because the RFC gives it the
+meaning "the element after the last", which never exists for a read. On an object only own members
+resolve, checked with `Object.hasOwn`, so `/toString` or `/__proto__` fail on a document that does
+not declare them while a document that does declare them (as `JSON.parse` produces) resolves them
+normally. Reaching a primitive with tokens left over is `unresolved`.
+
+**Only the string form is accepted.** The URI fragment representation of § 6 (`#/items/0`,
+percent-encoded) is a different encoding layer; callers decode it before calling in. Accepting both
+in one function would make `#` ambiguous between a fragment marker and a member name.
+
+**Public surface stays at what is used.** `resolvePointer`, `JsonPointerError`,
+`JsonPointerErrorReason`, `JsonValue` and `JsonObject` are exported from `src/index.ts`. The pointer
+parser stays module-private until a feature outside the module needs it, rather than being exported
+on the expectation that one will.
+
+**Tooling: sources import each other with the `.ts` extension.** This lets `node --test` run the
+tests against the sources with no build step, which Node's native type stripping requires;
+`tsc` is configured with `allowImportingTsExtensions` and `rewriteRelativeImportExtensions` so the
+emitted JavaScript imports `.js` normally. `typescript` and `@types/node` are added as dev
+dependencies, with `npm run typecheck` (sources plus tests, via `tsconfig.test.json`) and
+`npm run build`; the library keeps no runtime dependency.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Later features (patching, merging, diffing) reuse `JsonPointerError` and its `reason`, and the
+  `JsonValue` type, instead of defining parallel ones.
+- `reason` is part of the published contract: adding a third reason is a breaking change for
+  callers that switch on it exhaustively.
+- Because values are returned in place, callers that must not disturb the document copy the result
+  themselves. This is documented in the README and in the function's doc comment.
+- Strict array indexing means a caller holding `"/items/01"` gets an error rather than
+  `items[1]`. That is what the RFC says, and the alternative would silently accept pointers other
+  conforming implementations reject.
+- Own-member lookup makes pointers safe against prototype-chain surprises, which matters because
+  pointers commonly arrive from outside the service.
+- A pointer that is both malformed and unresolvable reports `invalid-pointer`; a caller probing for
+  existence must therefore still treat `invalid-pointer` as a failure, not as "absent".
+- The `.ts`-extension import style is now a project convention: a new source file that imports a
+  sibling must use it, or `node --test` stops working on the sources.
