+---
+id: task-001-implement-json-pointer-resolvepointer-rfc-6901
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Pointer resolvePointer (RFC 6901)"
+status: approved
+---
+
+# Implement JSON Pointer `resolvePointer` (RFC 6901)
+
+## Request
+
+Give the services a standard way to refer to a value inside a JSON document: implement JSON Pointer
+as specified in RFC 6901, exporting `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` from `src/index.ts`. It
+returns the value the pointer refers to, and throws when the pointer is not a valid JSON Pointer or
+does not resolve to a value of the document. It is the first of several features that will build on
+it.
+
+## What was done
+
+Test first, then the implementation: `test/json-pointer.test.ts` was written and run against a
+missing module (red), then the module was added until the suite passed (green).
+
+- `src/json-value.ts` — the `JsonValue` / `JsonObject` types shared by the whole library.
+- `src/json-pointer.ts` — `resolvePointer`, `JsonPointerError`, `JsonPointerErrorReason`, and a
+  module-private `parsePointer` that validates and splits a pointer into unescaped reference tokens.
+- `src/index.ts` — the public surface, re-exporting those.
+- `test/json-pointer.test.ts` — 20 tests, happy and error paths.
+- `README.md` — a feature section with usage, the two failure modes, and the two points of the RFC
+  that are easy to get wrong.
+- `tsconfig.json`, `tsconfig.test.json`, `package.json` — `.ts`-extension imports so `node --test`
+  runs the sources with no build step, plus `typecheck` and `build` scripts and the `typescript` /
+  `@types/node` dev dependencies. No runtime dependency was added.
+
+Behaviour, following RFC 6901 § 3 (syntax) and § 4 (evaluation):
+
+- the empty pointer returns the document itself; otherwise a pointer must start with `/` and every
+  `~` must be followed by `0` or `1`;
+- tokens are unescaped `~1` before `~0`, so `~01` decodes to `~1`;
+- on an array, only the § 4 `array-index` form resolves (`0`, or digits with no leading zero); `-`,
+  `01` and `length` do not, and an out-of-range index does not;
+- on an object, only own members resolve (`Object.hasOwn`), so `/toString` and `/__proto__` fail
+  unless the document really declares them;
+- reaching a primitive or `null` with tokens left over does not resolve;
+- the value is returned in place, not copied.
+
+Failures throw `JsonPointerError` with `reason: 'invalid-pointer' | 'unresolved'`. The message names
+the pointer, the location reached and what was wrong, e.g.
+`JSON Pointer "/a/b" does not resolve: the value at "/a" has no member "b".`
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 20 tests, 20 pass, 0 fail.
+- `npm run typecheck` — clean, sources and tests, `strict` on.
+- `npm run build` — clean; the emitted `dist/index.js` was imported and exercised, confirming the
+  `.ts` → `.js` specifier rewriting works.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- `dl-001-shape-of-the-json-pointer-api-errors-types-and-strictness` — error signalling, the shared
+  `JsonValue` type, returning values in place, strict RFC evaluation, the string-form-only scope,
+  the minimal public surface, and the `.ts`-extension import convention.
+
+## Follow-up for the next features
+
+- `parsePointer` is module-private; export it from `src/index.ts` when a feature outside
+  `src/json-pointer.ts` needs the token list.
+- The features to come that write into a document will want to resolve a pointer's *parent* and to
+  give `-` its "append" meaning (RFC 6902); the token list and `JsonPointerError.reason` are the
+  pieces they build on.
